      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Our Story</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          Wagecheck was built because understanding your take-home pay should not require a degree in accounting. Too many UK workers rely on rough estimates or outdated assumptions when making financial decisions — from accepting a job offer to planning a mortgage. We wanted to create a tool that is fast, accurate and genuinely free. No sign-ups, no paywalls, no hidden agendas. Just honest calculators that give you the numbers you need to make confident decisions about your money.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          The project started with a simple salary calculator and grew into a comprehensive suite of 17 tools covering income tax, National Insurance, stamp duty, self-employment, sick pay and more. Every calculator runs entirely in your browser — your salary data never leaves your device.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">How We Calculate</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          Transparency is central to everything we do. Every calculation on Wagecheck uses the exact same rules published by HMRC, Revenue Scotland and the Welsh Revenue Authority. We apply the correct income tax bands, National Insurance thresholds, pension auto-enrolment rules and student loan repayment plans for the current tax year. Where a calculator makes assumptions — for example, a 5% pension contribution or a specific tax code — we state those assumptions clearly on the page.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          We do not round figures to make them look cleaner. The numbers you see are the actual calculated values, rounded only to the nearest pound where appropriate. If your payslip shows a slightly different figure, it is usually because your employer uses a different pay frequency or applies employer-specific deductions that our general-purpose calculator cannot account for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Data Sources</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
          All tax rates and thresholds on Wagecheck come from official UK government sources:
